      'description': "Upload a file, sent as base64 content, to a document as a new version. The new version becomes the newest one: from then on the document's file, download and text are the new file's, while title, tags and other metadata stay and the older versions are kept. The type comes from the content, not the name: PDF, images, plain text and CSV (Office, OpenDocument and RTF only if Paperless runs Tika). Paperless consumes the file later: the result holds the task_id and, with wait_seconds, the version_id; a problem found only then shows as state failure. A file Paperless already holds (same bytes) is not sent without confirm: first a dialog, or an error for you to show the user, names where it is (see confirm). Checked are the originals of documents, the versions of the 5,000 last changed and 500 trash entries; not archive files and what this user cannot see (duplicates.not_checked says more). Needs the permissions to add and change documents, and the user must own the document, hold a change grant on it, or be a superuser, or the document must be unowned.",
